WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.072
Throughput versus latency, and continuous batching.

2
00:00:03.352 --> 00:00:09.432
Throughput is how much work the server completes per unit time (tokens per second, or tasks per hour).

3
00:00:09.712 --> 00:00:12.229
Latency is how long one request waits.

4
00:00:12.509 --> 00:00:14.301
Batching trades one for the other.

5
00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:25.568
Put more sequences into each decode step and throughput rises, because the weight reads are shared; but each request now waits for a step that does more work, and new requests may queue.

6
00:00:25.848 --> 00:00:32.589
Older servers used static batching: collect a batch, run it until every sequence finishes, then start the next.

7
00:00:32.869 --> 00:00:35.856
Short requests sat idle waiting for the longest one.

8
00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:44.904
Continuous batching schedules at the level of individual decode steps: when a sequence finishes, a waiting request takes its slot at the next step.

9
00:00:45.184 --> 00:00:47.445
Every current engine in this lesson does this.

10
00:00:47.725 --> 00:00:53.699
The other big idea came from the vLLM paper (Kwon and colleagues, 2023).

11
00:00:53.979 --> 00:01:02.619
Allocating each request’s KV cache as one contiguous block wastes memory, because you must reserve space for the longest possible output.

12
00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:12.477
PagedAttention stores the KV cache in fixed-size blocks, like pages in an operating system’s virtual memory, allocated as the sequence grows.

13
00:01:12.757 --> 00:01:15.232
Less waste means more sequences in flight.

14
00:01:15.512 --> 00:01:24.173
The paper reports two to four times the throughput of the systems it compared against at similar latency, with larger gains for longer sequences.

15
00:01:24.453 --> 00:01:34.587
The practical consequences for your benchmark: throughput is not a property of the model, it is a property of the model, the hardware, the engine, the batch settings, and the traffic.

16
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:38.984
Measure it under the concurrency you expect, and report it with those settings.

17
00:01:39.264 --> 00:01:43.445
The cost-per-task number from lesson 10 is only as good as that measurement.
